@startuml per-store-idempotency
title US-11: Per-Store Idempotency - Same Key, Different Mechanism

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Position\nConsumer" as Consumer
database "TimescaleDB\nposition_history" as TSDB
database "Neo4j\nentity graph" as Neo4j
database "Redis\nentity:live:{id}" as Redis

Consumer -> Consumer : derive idempotency key\n{entity_id}:{timestamp_ms}

Consumer -> TSDB : INSERT INTO position_history\n(entity_id, timestamp_ms, lat, lon, geo_cell)\nON CONFLICT (entity_id, timestamp_ms) DO NOTHING

note right of TSDB
  Duplicate row silently ignored.
  Conflict target is the composite
  primary key (entity_id, timestamp_ms).
end note

Consumer -> Neo4j : MERGE (a)-[e:PROXIMITY_EVENT {\n  idempotency_key: $key\n}]->(b)\nON CREATE SET e.lat = $lat, e.lon = $lon

note right of Neo4j
  MERGE matches on idempotency_key.
  If edge exists - no-op.
  If edge absent - create it.
  Never produces duplicate edges.
end note

Consumer -> Redis : HSET entity:live:{entity_id}\nlat $lat lon $lon timestamp_ms $ts

note right of Redis
  HSET overwrites the field value.
  Writing the same key/value twice
  has no observable side effect.
  Naturally idempotent.
end note

@enduml
